{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And one who steals a man.</b> Why is this written here, between one who strikes his father and one who curses his father? Because one who strikes his father and one who steals a man have the same death penalty — strangulation. But one who curses his father — his death penalty is stoning, as it is written in the parashah of Kedoshim: ״אביו ואמו קלל דמיו בו.״ And even though it would seem that cursing is lighter, since one who curses does no deed and one who strikes does a deed — even so, cursing is punished by stoning, which is more severe than strangulation. For it is established to us that his honor is likened to the honor of the Place [i.e. HaShem], and 'cursing' applies to HaShem, since one can curse HaShem, and therefore he is punished by stoning, like one who curses HaShem. But 'striking' does not apply to HaShem, and therefore his death penalty is strangulation. Furthermore, one who curses after death is liable, but one who strikes after death is exempt, because he is liable only for a blow that involves a wound, and a wound does not exist after death. And it is difficult for me, according to what was explained, that one who strikes his father and mother is stoned because striking does not apply to HaShem — if so, cursing after death, which does not apply to HaShem, should not be liable to stoning. And perhaps one may say: since the name 'cursing' exists regarding the Name, we do not distinguish between cursing during life and cursing after death. And further, one may say a reason why it is written וגונב איש between striking and cursing: because through being stolen in his youth, he does not recognize his father and mother and curses them and strikes them. But one must consider: why is איש written here, which implies an adult and not a minor? One may say: it is written elsewhere: ״כי ימצא איש גונב נפש מאחיו.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The phrase 'דמנא לן' in parentheses appears in the source; translated literally as 'that we learn.' The word השם in the source is rendered HaShem per voice rules; the honor of המקום ('the Place') is a rabbinic epithet for HaShem and translated literally as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:47.254337436Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}